\title{The Geometry of the Retention Knee:\\ Grids, Majorization, and a Collision-Entropy Dichotomy\\ for Top-$k$ Attention Budgets}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Limited-memory deployment of attention-based sequence models rests on a single scalar: the \emph{retention knee} $k^*(g)$, the least number of top-weighted keys whose retained attention mass reaches a gate $g$. We develop a complete, assumption-explicit theory of this quantity for an arbitrary nonnegative weight profile, and use it to audit a concrete experimental reading --- a fine sweep at context $2048$, gate $g=0.98$, over the grid $\{20,24,28,32\}$ with retained masses $0.9793,\,0.9835,\,0.9854,\,0.9885$.

Four groups of results are established. (1) \emph{Order and grid geometry}: the knee is well posed and characterised by a fail/pass certificate; a sweep restricted to a grid $G$ never under-reports ($k^*\le k^*_G$), refining a grid can only lower the report, an on-grid knee is reported exactly, and on an arithmetic grid of spacing $s$ one has $k^*\le k^*_G<k^*+s$. The reported ``$24$'' is exactly the bracket $20<k^*(0.98)\le 24$. (2) \emph{Majorization}: a profile majorized in the partial-sum order has a knee at least as large; the strict variant forces the deployment chain $16<20<24$ rather than fitting it, and the chain is realizable by honest sorted profiles. (3) \emph{A negative result}: for sorted rows the retention curve is discretely concave and window averaging preserves this, but the reported four numbers violate discrete concavity, so they cannot be window-averaged top-$k$ masses of sorted rows; the knee conclusion survives, concavity-based extrapolation does not. (4) \emph{A collision-entropy floor and its exact scope}: Cauchy--Schwarz gives $g^2\le k\,E(k)$ with $E(k)=\sum_{i<k}w_i^2$ the attention energy ($2^{-H_2}$), hence $k^*(g)\ge g^2/E$; read backwards a certified knee $k^*\le K$ forces $E(K)\ge g^2/K$, giving the falsifiable prediction $E(24)>0.04$, i.e.\ $H_2<\log_2 25<4.65$ bits. The floor is attained on plateau profiles. On geometric rows $w_i=(1-a)a^i$ the knee-to-floor ratio is bounded by $(1+\log\frac1{1-g})/g^2$, a gate-only constant (below $6$ at $g=0.98$), refuting the natural conjecture that it diverges as $a\to1^-$; on spike-plus-plateau rows the same ratio is unbounded at fixed gate. Exponential decay, not sortedness, is what makes an entropy measurement predictive of a key budget. Combining floor and geometric-tail ceiling pins the knee two-sidedly and yields a consistency test $g^2/E\le N$ on any reported (gate, energy, tail) triple.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} retention knee; top-$k$ attention; collision entropy; R\'enyi-2; Cauchy--Schwarz; majorization; discrete concavity; grid refinement; key--value cache budget.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The object of study}

Let $w:\N\to\R$ be a nonnegative weight profile --- in the motivating application, a row of an attention matrix sorted nonincreasingly, so $w_0\ge w_1\ge\cdots\ge0$ and $\sum_i w_i=1$. Define the \emph{retained mass}
\[
M_w(k)=\sum_{i<k}w_i,
\]
the total attention weight carried by the $k$ heaviest keys, and for a \emph{gate} $g\in(0,1)$ the \emph{retention knee}
\[
k^*_w(g)=\min\{k\in\N:\ M_w(k)\ge g\},
\]
with $k^*=0$ when the defining set is empty; all substantive statements carry an explicit attainability hypothesis $\exists k,\ g\le M_w(k)$.

The knee is the scalar that limited-memory deployment tables report: at context $C$, keep $k^*$ keys and retain a fraction $g$ of attention mass. It controls the key--value cache size, hence memory footprint.

\subsection{The experimental reading being audited}

The data set motivating this development is a fine sweep at context $2048$, gate $g=0.98$ exact, averaged over $12$ evaluation windows:

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{lcccc}
\toprule
$k$ & $20$ & $24$ & $28$ & $32$\\
\midrule
retained & $0.9793$ (fail) & $0.9835$ (pass) & $0.9854$ (pass) & $0.9885$ (pass)\\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

together with the deployment chain: knees $16,20,24$ at contexts $512,1024,2048$, all inside a $\approx30$-key budget. A previous coarser sweep had reported $28$ at context $2048$. Our aim is not to re-derive these numbers but to determine exactly what they entail: what is \emph{proved} (the bracket, the chain), what is \emph{predicted} (an entropy ceiling on the rows), and what is \emph{refuted} (the implicit concave model).

\section{Definitions and basic structure}

\begin{definition}[Retained mass]
$M_w(k)=\sum_{i<k}w_i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee]
$k^*_w(g)=\inf\{k\in\N: g\le M_w(k)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Attention energy]
$E_w(k)=\sum_{i<k}w_i^2$. For a probability profile this is the \emph{collision probability}, $E=2^{-H_2}$ with $H_2$ the R\'enyi-2 entropy.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Grid knee]
For $G\subseteq\N$ a set of tested key counts, $k^*_{w,G}(g)=\inf\{k: k\in G\text{ and } g\le M_w(k)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Window average]
For rows $W_0,\dots,W_{m-1}$, $\overline M(k)=\frac1m\sum_{j<m}M_{W_j}(k)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Model profiles]\leavevmode
\begin{itemize}[nosep]
\item \emph{Plateau (step) profile} $S_{K,c}(i)=c$ for $i<K$, $0$ otherwise.
\item \emph{Geometric row} $\gamma_a(i)=(1-a)a^i$, $a\in[0,1)$: a probability profile.
\item \emph{Spike-plus-plateau row} $\sigma_m(0)=\tfrac12$, $\sigma_m(i)=\tfrac1{4m}$ for $1\le i\le 2m$, $0$ beyond: a sorted probability profile for $m\ge1$.
\end{itemize}
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Elementary properties]\label{lem:elem}
If $w\ge0$ then $M_w$ is nondecreasing; $E_w$ is nondecreasing for any $w$; $M_w(k+1)=M_w(k)+w_k$; and $M$ is linear in the profile, $M_{au+bv}=aM_u+bM_v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Immediate: monotonicity is summation over a larger index set with nonnegative summands. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Block increments of a sorted row]\label{lem:block}
If $w$ is antitone and $k\le k'$ then for every $d$,
\[
M_w(k'+d)-M_w(k')\ \le\ M_w(k+d)-M_w(k).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Both sides are $d$-term sums $\sum_{j<d}w_{k'+j}$ and $\sum_{j<d}w_{k+j}$; termwise $w_{k'+j}\le w_{k+j}$ since $k+j\le k'+j$. \end{proof}

Lemma~\ref{lem:block} is the \emph{discrete concavity} of the retention curve.

\section{Well-posedness and grid geometry}

\begin{proposition}[Pass/knee duality]\label{prop:duality}
(i) $g\le M_w(k)\Rightarrow k^*_w(g)\le k$. (ii) If the gate is attainable then $g\le M_w(k^*_w(g))$. (iii) $k<k^*_w(g)\Rightarrow M_w(k)<g$. (iv) For $w\ge0$ with attainable gate, $g\le M_w(k)\iff k^*_w(g)\le k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(i),(ii) are the defining properties of the infimum of a nonempty set of naturals; (iii) is the contrapositive of (i); (iv) combines (i) with monotonicity. \end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Fail/pass certificate]\label{prop:cert}
If $w\ge0$, $k\ge1$ and $M_w(k-1)<g\le M_w(k)$, then $k^*_w(g)=k$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
$\le$ is Proposition~\ref{prop:duality}(i). If $k^*_w(g)\le k-1$, monotonicity and Proposition~\ref{prop:duality}(ii) give $g\le M_w(k-1)$, contradicting failure. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Bracketing]\label{thm:bracket}
If $w\ge0$ and $M_w(a)<g\le M_w(b)$ then $a<k^*_w(g)\le b$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $k^*_w(g)\le a$, monotonicity forces $g\le M_w(a)$. The upper bound is Proposition~\ref{prop:duality}(i). \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The round-16 reading, exactly]\label{cor:reading}
Any nonnegative profile with $M_w(20)=0.9793$, $M_w(24)=0.9835$ satisfies $20<k^*_w(0.98)\le 24$. The margin structure is $M(24)-0.98=0.0035$ and $0.98-M(20)=0.0007$: the pass margin is exactly five times the fail deficit.
\end{corollary}

The knee is also monotone in the gate: $g_1\le g_2\Rightarrow k^*_w(g_1)\le k^*_w(g_2)$ whenever $g_2$ is attainable.

\subsection{What a sweep can report}

\begin{theorem}[No under-reporting]\label{thm:nounder}
If some $k\in G$ passes then $k^*_w(g)\le k^*_{w,G}(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The grid knee passes the gate; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:duality}(i). \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Refinement lowers the report]\label{thm:refine}
If $G\subseteq G'$ and some $k\in G$ passes then $k^*_{w,G'}(g)\le k^*_{w,G}(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The $G$-minimizer lies in $G'$ and passes, hence is a candidate for the $G'$-infimum. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[On-grid landing]\label{thm:ongrid}
If $k^*_w(g)\in G$ and the gate is attainable then $k^*_{w,G}(g)=k^*_w(g)$. If $k^*_w(g)\notin G$ and some grid point passes then $k^*_w(g)<k^*_{w,G}(g)$ strictly.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The true knee is an admissible grid candidate, giving $\le$; Theorem~\ref{thm:nounder} gives $\ge$. Conversely equality would place the true knee in $G$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative grid bias]\label{thm:gridbias}
Let $w\ge0$, $s\ge1$, and let $G$ contain $\{a+sj: j\in\N\}$ with $a\le k^*_w(g)$, the gate attainable. Then
\[
k^*_w(g)\ \le\ k^*_{w,G}(g)\ <\ k^*_w(g)+s.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $j$ with $k^*_w(g)-a\le sj<(k^*_w(g)-a)+s$. Then $a+sj\ge k^*_w(g)$ is a grid point, so it passes by monotonicity, giving $k^*_{w,G}(g)\le a+sj<k^*_w(g)+s$; the left inequality is Theorem~\ref{thm:nounder}. \end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Theorems~\ref{thm:nounder}--\ref{thm:gridbias} dissolve the apparent conflict between a coarse sweep reporting $28$ and a fine sweep reporting $24$: refinement can only lower the report, and a grid of spacing $s$ localizes the truth only to a window of width $s$. Spacing $4$ yields $20<k^*\le24$; spacing $16$ yields nothing better than a $16$-wide window.
\end{remark}

\begin{example}[A genuine coarse-grid over-provision]\label{ex:dyadicgrid}
Let $w_i=2^{-(i+1)}$, so $M_w(k)=1-2^{-k}$. Then $M_w(5)=0.96875<0.98\le0.984375=M_w(6)$, so $k^*_w(0.98)=6$. On the grid $\{2,4,8,16\}$ the sweep reports $8$: a $33\%$ over-provision. Adjoining the point $6$ recovers the truth.
\end{example}

\section{Majorization: why the deployment chain climbs}

Say $w$ \emph{majorizes} $v$ in the partial-sum order if $M_v(k)\le M_w(k)$ for all $k$: informally, $v$ is more spread out.

\begin{theorem}[Majorization implies knee monotonicity]\label{thm:maj}
If $M_v(k)\le M_w(k)$ for all $k$ and the gate is attainable for $v$, then $k^*_w(g)\le k^*_v(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$g\le M_v(k^*_v(g))\le M_w(k^*_v(g))$; apply Proposition~\ref{prop:duality}(i). \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Strict version]\label{thm:majstrict}
Let $v\ge0$ with attainable gate. If $M_v(k^*_w(g))<g$ then $k^*_w(g)<k^*_v(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise $k^*_v(g)\le k^*_w(g)$ and monotonicity gives $g\le M_v(k^*_v(g))\le M_v(k^*_w(g))<g$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The deployment chain, derived]\label{thm:chain}
Let $w_{512},w_{1024},w_{2048}\ge0$ satisfy
\[
M_{512}(15)<g\le M_{512}(16),\quad M_{1024}(19)<g\le M_{1024}(20),\quad M_{2048}(23)<g\le M_{2048}(24).
\]
Then $k^*_{512}(g)=16$, $k^*_{1024}(g)=20$, $k^*_{2048}(g)=24$; the chain is strictly increasing; and $k^*_{2048}(g)\le30$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Three applications of Proposition~\ref{prop:cert}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The chain is forced, not fitted]\label{thm:forced}
If each longer-context profile is nonnegative with attainable gate and still fails the gate at the previous context's knee, then $k^*_{512}(g)<k^*_{1024}(g)<k^*_{2048}(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Two applications of Theorem~\ref{thm:majstrict}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Realizability]\label{thm:realiz}
For every gate $g>0$ and $K\ge1$ the plateau $S_{K,g/K}$ is nonnegative, antitone, satisfies $M(K-1)<g\le M(K)$ and has $k^*(g)=K$. Hence there are honest sorted profiles realizing the certificates of Theorem~\ref{thm:chain} at $K=16,20,24$ with $g=0.98$: the chain is not vacuous.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$M_{S_{K,c}}(k)=c\min(k,K)$; with $c=g/K$ this is $g\frac{K-1}{K}<g$ at $k=K-1$ and exactly $g$ at $k=K$. Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:cert}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Mixtures: averaging heads never costs keys]\label{thm:mix}
For $u,v\ge0$ with attainable gates and $\lambda\in[0,1]$,
\[
k^*_{\lambda u+(1-\lambda)v}(g)\ \le\ \max\{k^*_u(g),k^*_v(g)\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $K$ the maximum, monotonicity gives $g\le M_u(K)$ and $g\le M_v(K)$; linearity (Lemma~\ref{lem:elem}) gives $M_{\lambda u+(1-\lambda)v}(K)\ge g$. \end{proof}

\section{A concavity obstruction: what the reported row cannot be}

\begin{theorem}[Window averaging preserves discrete concavity]\label{thm:avgconc}
For antitone rows $W_0,\dots,W_{m-1}$ with window average $\overline M$, and $k\le k'$, $d\ge0$,
\[
\overline M(k'+d)-\overline M(k')\ \le\ \overline M(k+d)-\overline M(k).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Sum Lemma~\ref{lem:block} over $j$ and divide by $m$ (trivial for $m=0$). \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Obstruction]\label{thm:obstruction}
There is no $m\in\N$ and no family of antitone rows whose window-averaged retention curve takes the values
\[
\overline M(20)=0.9793,\quad \overline M(24)=0.9835,\quad \overline M(28)=0.9854,\quad \overline M(32)=0.9885.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:avgconc} with $k=24$, $k'=28$, $d=4$ requires $\overline M(32)-\overline M(28)\le \overline M(28)-\overline M(24)$, i.e.\ $0.0031\le0.0019$, false. \end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is a modelling refutation, not an empirical one. What fails is the identification of the reported numbers with window-averaged top-$k$ masses of sorted rows. Survives: everything in Sections 3--4, which use only monotonicity and the fail/pass pattern; in particular $20<k^*(0.98)\le24$ and the strict chain. Fails: any extrapolation premised on a concave retention curve --- interpolating $k^*$ at a finer gate, fitting a concave curve between grid points, or projecting a larger model's cell from a smaller model's curve.
\end{remark}

\section{The collision-entropy floor}

\begin{theorem}[Cauchy--Schwarz for the retention curve]\label{thm:cs}
If $g\ge0$ and $g\le M_w(k)$ then $g^2\le k\,E_w(k)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Cauchy--Schwarz against the all-ones vector gives $M_w(k)^2\le k E_w(k)$, and $0\le g\le M_w(k)$ gives $g^2\le M_w(k)^2$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy floor for the knee]\label{thm:floor}
If $g\ge0$, $E>0$, $E_w(k)\le E$ for all $k$, and the gate is attainable, then $g^2/E\le k^*_w(g)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} at $k=k^*_w(g)$: $g^2\le k^*E_w(k^*)\le k^*E$. \end{proof}

Since $E=2^{-H_2}$ for a probability row: a row of collision entropy at least $H_2$ needs at least $g^2 2^{H_2}$ keys.

\begin{theorem}[Backward reading: a measured knee caps the entropy]\label{thm:back}
If $w\ge0$, $g\ge0$, $K\ge1$, the gate is attainable and $k^*_w(g)\le K$, then $E_w(K)\ge g^2/K$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$g\le M_w(k^*)\le M_w(K)$ by monotonicity; apply Theorem~\ref{thm:cs} at $k=K$. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[A falsifiable prediction from the round-16 reading]\label{cor:predict}
Any nonnegative attention profile with $k^*_w(0.98)\le24$ satisfies $E_w(24)>0.04$, i.e.\ $H_2<\log_2 25\approx4.644$ bits over those keys.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$E_w(24)\ge 0.98^2/24=0.9604/24=0.0400166\ldots>0.04$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Sharpness]\label{thm:sharp}
The plateau $S_{24,\,0.98/24}$ is nonnegative and antitone, has knee exactly $24$ at gate $0.98$, and energy exactly $0.98^2/24$. Hence the constant of Corollary~\ref{cor:predict} cannot be increased, and its hypotheses are satisfiable.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$E_{S_{K,c}}(k)=c^2\min(k,K)$, so $E(24)=(0.98/24)^2\cdot24=0.98^2/24$; the knee is $24$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:realiz}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Peak bound]\label{thm:peak}
If $w_i\le M$ for all $i$ with $M>0$ and the gate is attainable, then $k^*_w(g)\ge g/M$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$g\le M_w(k^*)\le k^*M$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Near-sharpness on the uniform row]\label{thm:unif}
For $u=S_{n,1/n}$ one has $E_u(n)=1/n$; the floor reads $k^*_u(g)\ge g^2 n$ while the truth is $k^*_u(g)\ge gn$. The floor is lossy by exactly one factor of the gate.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$E_u(n)=(1/n)^2n=1/n$. Since $M_u(k)\le k/n$, passing the gate forces $k\ge gn$. The floor is Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} with $E=1/n$. \end{proof}

At $g=0.98$ the loss factor is $1/g=1.0204$.

\begin{theorem}[Two-sided sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
Suppose $E_w(k)\le E$ for all $k$ with $E>0$, and $1-M_w(k)\le Cr^k$ for all $k$. If $Cr^N\le 1-g$ then
\[
\frac{g^2}{E}\ \le\ k^*_w(g)\ \le\ N.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$1-M_w(N)\le Cr^N\le1-g$ gives $M_w(N)\ge g$, hence $k^*\le N$; the same computation supplies attainability for Theorem~\ref{thm:floor}. \end{proof}

An exponentially decaying tail ($r<1$, $C>0$, $g<1$) always yields a finite knee, since $r^N<(1-g)/C$ for large $N$; the ``$\approx30$ keys'' budget is the instance $N=30$.

\begin{corollary}[Consistency test]\label{cor:consist}
Any reported triple (gate $g$, energy bound $E$, tail certificate $Cr^N\le1-g$) must satisfy $g^2/E\le N$. A report violating this is internally inconsistent whatever the sweep says.
\end{corollary}

At $g=0.98$, $N=30$ the test demands $E\ge0.032$; the round-16 reading, which forces $E\ge0.0400$, clears it.

\section{How lossy is the floor? The geometric family}

\begin{lemma}[Closed-form retention]\label{lem:geomass}
$M_{\gamma_a}(k)=1-a^k$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
Induction: $M(k+1)=(1-a^k)+(1-a)a^k=1-a^{k+1}$. \end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Exact energy bound]\label{lem:geoenergy}
For $a\in[0,1)$ and every $k$, $E_{\gamma_a}(k)\le \Egeo(a):=\frac{1-a}{1+a}$, and $\Egeo(a)>0$ is the exact total energy.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$E_{\gamma_a}(k)=(1-a)^2\frac{1-a^{2k}}{1-a^2}\le\frac{(1-a)^2}{1-a^2}=\frac{1-a}{1+a}$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Power certificate]\label{thm:geocert}
$a^N\le1-g$ implies $k^*_{\gamma_a}(g)\le N$; conversely for $a<1$, $g<1$ one has $a^{k^*_{\gamma_a}(g)}\le1-g$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:geomass}, $M(N)\ge g\iff a^N\le1-g$; attainability holds since $a^N\to0$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Logarithmic knee ceiling]\label{thm:geolog}
For $0<a<1$, $0\le g<1$,
\[
k^*_{\gamma_a}(g)\ \le\ 1+\frac{\log\frac1{1-g}}{1-a}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $L=\log\frac1{1-g}\ge0$ and $N=\lceil L/(1-a)\rceil$. Since $\log a\le a-1$, $-\log a\ge1-a$, so $N(-\log a)\ge N(1-a)\ge L$, i.e.\ $N\log a\le\log(1-g)$, i.e.\ $a^N\le1-g$. Theorem~\ref{thm:geocert} gives $k^*\le N<L/(1-a)+1$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Floor for geometric rows]\label{thm:geofloor}
For $0\le a<1$, $0\le g<1$,
\[
\frac{g^2}{\Egeo(a)}=\frac{g^2(1+a)}{1-a}\ \le\ k^*_{\gamma_a}(g).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:floor} with $E=\Egeo(a)$, admissible by Lemma~\ref{lem:geoenergy}. \end{proof}

Both bounds diverge like $1/(1-a)$ as $a\to1^-$. It is natural to conjecture that the ratio (truth)/(floor) also diverges. It does not.

\begin{theorem}[Flatness bound; refutation of the blow-up conjecture]\label{thm:flat}
For every $a\in(0,1)$ and $g\in(0,1)$,
\[
k^*_{\gamma_a}(g)\ \le\ \frac{1+\log\frac1{1-g}}{g^2}\cdot\frac{g^2}{\Egeo(a)} .
\]
Hence there is $C(g)>0$, independent of $a$, with $k^*_{\gamma_a}(g)\le C(g)g^2/\Egeo(a)$ for all geometric rows: the knee-to-floor ratio does not blow up as $a\to1^-$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
With $L=\log\frac1{1-g}\ge0$ the right side equals $(1+L)(1+a)/(1-a)$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:geolog} it suffices that $1+\frac{L}{1-a}\le\frac{(1+L)(1+a)}{1-a}$, i.e.\ $(1-a)+L\le(1+L)(1+a)=1+a+L+La$, i.e.\ $-a\le a+La$, true. \end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Explicit constant at the round-16 gate]\label{cor:six}
For every $a\in(0,1)$, $k^*_{\gamma_a}(0.98)\le 6\cdot\frac{0.98^2}{\Egeo(a)}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
$\log 50<4$ since $e^4>50$, so $C(0.98)=(1+\log50)/0.9604<5/0.9604<6$. \end{proof}

\begin{example}[Dyadic row]\label{ex:dyadic}
For $a=1/2$: $\Egeo=1/3$; the knee at gate $0.98$ is exactly $6$; the floor is $0.9604\times3=2.8812$; the ratio is $2.08$, comfortably inside $6$.
\end{example}

\section{The dichotomy: where the floor fails}

\begin{lemma}[$\sigma_m$ is an honest sorted probability row]\label{lem:spike}
For $m\ge1$, $\sigma_m\ge0$ is antitone and $\sum_i\sigma_m(i)=\tfrac12+2m\cdot\tfrac1{4m}=1$, with
\[
M_{\sigma_m}(k)=\tfrac12+\tfrac{k-1}{4m}\qquad(1\le k\le 2m+1).
\]
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Knee grows linearly in plateau length]\label{thm:spikeknee}
For $m\ge1$, $k^*_{\sigma_m}(3/4)=m+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$M_{\sigma_m}(m+1)=\tfrac12+\tfrac{m}{4m}=\tfrac34$ exactly, while $M_{\sigma_m}(m)=\tfrac12+\tfrac{m-1}{4m}<\tfrac34$. Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:cert}. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Energy is pinned]\label{thm:spikeenergy}
For $m\ge1$: $E_{\sigma_m}(k)\ge\tfrac14$ for $k\ge1$, and $E_{\sigma_m}(k)\le\tfrac14+\tfrac1{8m}$ for all $k$. In particular the R\'enyi-2 entropy never exceeds $2$ bits, however long the plateau.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$E(1)=1/4$ and $E$ is monotone; the total energy is $\tfrac14+2m(\tfrac1{4m})^2=\tfrac14+\tfrac1{8m}$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Floor bounded while the knee is not]\label{thm:spikefloor}
For $m\ge1$, $\dfrac{(3/4)^2}{\tfrac14+\tfrac1{8m}}\le\dfrac94$, whereas $k^*_{\sigma_m}(3/4)=m+1$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\frac{9/16}{1/4+1/(8m)}\le\frac{9/16}{1/4}=\frac94$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded loss]\label{thm:unbounded}
For every $R\in\R$ there are a nonnegative antitone probability row $w$ and $E>0$ with $E_w(k)\le E$ for all $k$, the gate $3/4$ attainable, and
\[
R\cdot\frac{(3/4)^2}{E}\ <\ k^*_w(3/4).
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Choose $m>\tfrac94|R|$, take $w=\sigma_m$, $E=\tfrac14+\tfrac1{8m}$. By Theorem~\ref{thm:spikefloor} the floor is at most $\tfrac94$, so $R(3/4)^2/E\le\tfrac94|R|<m<m+1=k^*_w(3/4)$. \end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tightness dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
At the gate $g=3/4$:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,label=(\arabic*)]
\item there is $C>0$ with $k^*_{\gamma_a}(3/4)\le C\frac{(3/4)^2}{\Egeo(a)}$ for every $a\in(0,1)$;
\item for every $C$ there is a sorted probability row $w$ with energy bound $E$ such that $C\frac{(3/4)^2}{E}<k^*_w(3/4)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
(1) is Theorem~\ref{thm:flat} at $g=3/4$; (2) is Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded}. \end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Exponential decay, not sortedness, is what makes the entropy floor informative. A collision-entropy measurement alone can never \emph{certify} a key budget: it gives a lower bound that may under-estimate by an arbitrary factor even for a perfectly sorted probability row at a fixed gate. The upper half of any honest bracket must come from a decay hypothesis (Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich}), and Corollary~\ref{cor:six} is a statement about the geometric family, not a universal law.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{A. Exact knee by prefix scan.} Sort nonincreasingly and accumulate until the running sum reaches $g$; return the index. Correctness: Proposition~\ref{prop:cert}. Cost $O(n\log n)$ (sort) $+O(n)$ (scan), $O(1)$ extra memory; $O(k^*)$ if pre-sorted.

\paragraph{B. Grid sweep with certified bracket.} For $G=\{k_1<\cdots<k_r\}$ evaluate $M(k_j)$ until the first pass at $k_j$; report the bracket $(k_{j-1},k_j]$. By Theorems~\ref{thm:nounder} and \ref{thm:gridbias} the report is never below the truth and, on an arithmetic grid of spacing $s$, never more than $s-1$ above. Optional bisection inside the bracket costs $O(\log s)$ further prefix evaluations.

\paragraph{C. Two-sided budget certificate.} Given an energy bound $E$ and a fitted tail $(C,r)$, report $\lceil g^2/E\rceil\le k^*\le N$ with $N$ least such that $Cr^N\le1-g$, and flag inconsistency if $g^2/E>N$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:consist}). Cost $O(n)$ for the energy and $O(\log_{1/r}\frac{C}{1-g})$ for $N$.

\section{Applications and discussion}

\paragraph{Cache provisioning.} The bracket, not the point estimate, is the deployable object; Theorem~\ref{thm:gridbias} converts grid spacing into an over-provision guarantee of at most $s-1$ keys.

\paragraph{Context scaling.} Theorem~\ref{thm:forced} converts a table of measurements into a structural prediction: if longer contexts spread attention, no context length may be cheaper than a shorter one, and measured non-monotonicity indicates a pipeline artifact.

\paragraph{Multi-head serving.} Theorem~\ref{thm:mix}: per-head budgets aggregate by a maximum, not a sum.

\paragraph{Entropy diagnostics, with a caveat.} Corollary~\ref{cor:predict} turns a knee measurement into a testable claim about row entropies, and Corollary~\ref{cor:six} shows entropy predicts the budget within a small constant on exponentially decaying rows. Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} delimits this: entropy alone is never a certificate, so a practical protocol measures both collision entropy (floor) and a tail fit (ceiling), reporting the sandwich of Theorem~\ref{thm:sandwich} and running the consistency test.

\paragraph{Auditing published tables.} Theorem~\ref{thm:obstruction} illustrates a cheap audit: equal-width block increments of a window-averaged sorted retention curve must be nonincreasing; a violation localizes an error in the measurement convention with no access to raw data.

\medskip

The theory separates into three registers with distinct hypotheses. \emph{Order-theoretic} results (Sections 3--4) need only $w\ge0$ and are robust to pipeline details. \emph{Shape-theoretic} results (Section 5) need antitone rows; this is where the reported row breaks, and it breaks exactly the inferences that need shape. \emph{Information-theoretic} results (Sections 6--8) need a second moment and are exactly as strong as Cauchy--Schwarz: sharp on plateaus, near-sharp on uniform rows, tight up to a gate-only constant on geometric rows, arbitrarily lossy on spike-plus-plateau rows.

Two conclusions deserve emphasis. First, a coarse--fine disagreement between sweeps is not empirical: refinement can only lower the report, so $28$ and $24$ are compatible readings of one row. Second, the natural blow-up conjecture for the entropy floor fails where one expects it to hold --- flattening an exponentially decaying row makes the knee and the floor diverge at the identical rate --- and holds where one might not expect it, namely when the row acquires a heavy spike over a long shelf. Flatness per se is not the enemy of the floor; a mixture of scales is.

\paragraph{Limitations.} The theory concerns a single fixed profile; sampling error across windows and the distribution of knees within a layer are out of scope. The tail hypothesis $1-M(k)\le Cr^k$ must be fitted and validated, not derived. The gate is fixed throughout; beyond monotonicity, the curve $g\mapsto k^*(g)$ is unconstrained by anything proved here without further shape hypotheses.

\section{Future directions}

\textbf{What survived.} All order-theoretic statements: gate monotonicity, the fail/pass certificate, the grid inequalities, majorization monotonicity, the geometric-tail budget, the entropy floor. The reported conclusion at context $2048$ survives as the bracket $20<k^*\le24$; nothing finer follows from a grid of spacing $4$.

\textbf{What failed.} The four numbers cannot be window-averaged top-$k$ masses of sorted rows, because their equal-width block increments increase at the last step. This is ``needs a different definition'', not ``false'': the knee statement is fine, but concavity-based extrapolation is unlicensed.

\textbf{A refuted conjecture.} On geometric rows the ratio (true knee)/(floor) was conjectured to grow without bound as $a\to1^-$; it is bounded by $(1+\log\frac1{1-g})/g^2$, since both quantities diverge at rate $1/(1-a)$.

\textbf{Open problems.}
\begin{enumerate}[nosep]
\item Fine sweeps on further corpora: a corpus whose coarse grid reported $32$ should be swept at spacing $4$; the refined report can only fall, and the outcome is decided within a window of four.
\item Domain-jump corpora: test the majorization hypothesis directly by comparing partial-sum curves, and predict the knee ordering without further sweeps.
\item Larger model cells: projecting budgets across model sizes requires shape assumptions unavailable for the current data; establishing concavity on raw rows is a prerequisite.
\item A tail-exponent measurement protocol: fit $(C,r)$, report the sandwich, and propagate the fit's uncertainty to the budget.
\item Interpolating families between the geometric and spike-plus-plateau extremes, to locate the structural threshold at which the knee-to-floor ratio ceases to be bounded --- presumably a scale-mixture or regular-variation index.
\item Quantization of the grid: study the distribution of $k^*\bmod s$ (the on-grid landing rate) as a diagnostic of how informative a fixed sweep grid is.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}

A single deployment-table entry --- ``$24$ keys at context $2048$'' --- resolves into its exact logical content: a bracket $20<k^*(0.98)\le24$ whose width is the grid spacing and no smaller; a chain $16<20<24$ forced by majorization rather than fitted; a prediction that the underlying rows carry collision entropy below $\log_2 25\approx4.64$ bits; an obstruction warning that the reported curve cannot be a window-averaged sorted retention curve; and a two-sided sandwich floored by Cauchy--Schwarz and capped by exponential decay --- a floor tight to a factor $\approx5$ on decaying rows at a $98\%$ gate and arbitrarily lossy on rows with a spike over a long shelf. Entropy bounds the budget from below, decay bounds it from above, and neither alone is the answer.

